\subsection{Refs that land on a trailer commit}
\label{sec:trailer-refs}

Tags and other refs are created by whatever is at HEAD when the
user asks, and HEAD is a trailer commit for as long as no new run
has started.
A tag created just after a run finished therefore points at a
commit that no listing contains.
Since a trailer commit exists only to complete one run, we treat
such a ref as naming that run, and answer with that run's header
commit.

Which run that is has to be read off the run id, the way every
other join in this chapter reads it (\cref{sec:run-index}).
The commit \emph{before} the trailer is not the pairing but a
coincidence of it, and it holds only while no two runs are in
flight at once---which is the very situation the two-commit
protocol was written to survive (\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}).
Let a logged program start another logged program and the inner
run finishes first, so the log reads
$H_\text{outer}$, $H_\text{inner}$, $T_\text{inner}$,
$T_\text{outer}$.
The commit before $T_\text{outer}$ is then $T_\text{inner}$, a
trailer commit that no listing contains, so a tag on the newest
commit resolves to something no caller can find and is lost to
every view.
Finish them the other way round---the log reads
$H_a$, $H_b$, $T_a$, $T_b$---and the commit before $T_a$ is
$H_b$: a real header, of the wrong run.
The student tags the run of [[a.py]] and playback shows them
[[b.py]].

The parent walk stays on as the fallback for a trailer commit the
index cannot place: one recorded without a run id, or one whose
header this history does not contain, as in a log assembled from
part of a bundle.
For those the topology is the only guess left, and it is the
guess that was correct back when a log could not hold overlapping
runs in the first place.

The hash reaching this function is whatever a ref or a caller
supplied, so the subject probe can fail, and its failing is not an
error here.
Git refuses a hash the object database no longer has, and a ref may
name an object it will not walk as a commit at all; the probe then
exits non-zero and [[git()]] would raise [[GitError]]
(\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
That failure is itself the answer to the question being asked: a hash
Git cannot read as a commit is certainly not a trailer commit.
So the probe opts out of [[check]] and such a hash resolves to
itself---the same answer the [[rev-parse]] probe below already gives
when it cannot step back to a parent.

Reporting \enquote{not a commit} to the caller instead, as [[None]] or
as an exception, has no shape that suits the callers.
Both of them are read paths that have to degrade rather than abort:
[[get_tag_map]] (\cref{sec:tag-resolution}), whose own
[[for-each-ref]] already falls back to an empty map, and [[play --from]]
(\cref{sec:play}), which turns a hash it cannot find in the commit
list into a message naming the ref the user typed.
Handing them back the hash unchanged leads each of them to the answer
it already has for a hash that names no run, whereas a second return
shape would give both of them a new failure mode to handle for
nothing gained.

The index is a parameter rather than something this function always
reads for itself, because [[get_tag_map]] resolves one hash per tag
and would otherwise walk the whole log once per tag to rebuild the
same map.
It builds the map once and hands it over; a caller with a single
hash to resolve, such as [[play --from]], omits the argument and
pays for the one walk it needs.

<<commits functions>>=
def resolve_record_commit(repo, commit_hash, records=None):
    """Return the commit that carries ``commit_hash``'s record.

    Trailer commits resolve to the header commit of the run they
    complete; every other hash resolves to itself, including one
    that Git will not read as a commit.  ``records`` is the map
    from :func:`get_record_commits`, read from ``repo`` when the
    caller supplies none.
    """
    probe = repo.git(
        "log", "-1", "--format=%s", commit_hash, check=False,
    )
    if probe.returncode != 0:
        return commit_hash
    if not probe.stdout.strip().startswith(TRAILER_PREFIX):
        return commit_hash

    if records is None:
        records = get_record_commits(repo)
    if commit_hash in records:
        return records[commit_hash]
    <<step back to the trailer's parent commit>>
@

<<step back to the trailer's parent commit>>=
parent = repo.git(
    "rev-parse", f"{commit_hash}^", check=False,
)
if parent.returncode != 0:
    return commit_hash
return parent.stdout.strip()
@

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_resolve_record_commit_maps_trailer_to_header(workdir):
    from learnlog.commits import resolve_record_commit
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    repo.begin_run(
        "hello.py", [], datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0),
    )
    header = repo.git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    trailer = repo.git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    assert resolve_record_commit(repo, trailer) == header
    assert resolve_record_commit(repo, header) == header
