\section{The view trace of a review run}
\label{sec:view-trace}

A review run records more than the fact that it happened: its trailer
carries one line per commit the student actually looked at
(\cref{gitrepo}), in the order they looked at them.

\begin{quote}
  [[Viewed: <commit hash> <ISO-8601 time> <action>]]
\end{quote}

Revisits appear again rather than being collapsed, because moving back
to a commit already seen is exactly the kind of comparison a reader of
the log wants to know happened.

Three bare tokens after the keyword is what makes this section cheap to
read back: hashes, timestamps and action words can contain neither a
space nor a newline, so splitting on whitespace is unambiguous and none
of the quoting that captured I/O needs applies here.

Parsing is forgiving.
A trailer written by a later version may carry actions or fields this
reader has never heard of, and a line truncated by a crash mid-write is
better dropped than turned into an exception in the middle of somebody's
listing---so a line that does not yield exactly three fields is skipped
rather than reported.

<<commits functions>>=
def parse_view_trace(block_text):
    """Return the view trace recorded in a run block.

    Reads the ``Viewed:`` lines of a review run's trailer and
    returns them in the order recorded, as
    ``(commit_hash, time, action)`` tuples.  A block without
    such lines---every ordinary program run---yields an empty
    list, and malformed lines are skipped.
    """
    views = []
    for line in block_text.split("\n"):
        if not line.startswith(VIEWED_FIELD):
            continue
        fields = line[len(VIEWED_FIELD):].split()
        if len(fields) == VIEWED_FIELD_COUNT:
            views.append(tuple(fields))
    return views
<<commits constants>>=
VIEWED_FIELD_COUNT = 3
@

The field name itself, [[VIEWED_FIELD]], is the writer's
(\cref{sec:finalising-a-run}) and arrives through the import above:
reader and writer sharing one spelling is what lets the parser trust
[[startswith]] here.

Let's verify that a whole navigation sequence---including the revisit
that makes it interesting---survives the trip through the trailer:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_parse_view_trace_reads_viewed_lines():
    from learnlog.commits import parse_view_trace
    first, second = "a" * 40, "b" * 40
    block = (
        "Run: learnlog play\n"
        "\n"
        "Script: learnlog\n"
        "Arguments: play\n"
        "Start-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:00\n"
        "Run-Id: abc123\n"
        "End-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:20\n"
        "Exit-Code: 0\n"
        f"Viewed: {first} 2026-01-01T10:00:01 open\n"
        f"Viewed: {second} 2026-01-01T10:00:05 next\n"
        f"Viewed: {first} 2026-01-01T10:00:09 prev\n"
        "\n--- I/O ---\n(no I/O)\n"
        "\n--- exception ---\nNone"
    )
    assert parse_view_trace(block) == [
        (first, "2026-01-01T10:00:01", "open"),
        (second, "2026-01-01T10:00:05", "next"),
        (first, "2026-01-01T10:00:09", "prev"),
    ]

def test_parse_view_trace_without_lines():
    """An ordinary program run has no view trace."""
    from learnlog.commits import parse_view_trace
    assert parse_view_trace(
        "Run: hello.py (none)\n\nScript: hello.py\n"
        "End-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:05\nExit-Code: 0\n"
    ) == []

def test_parse_view_trace_skips_malformed():
    """A truncated line is dropped, not reported."""
    from learnlog.commits import parse_view_trace
    assert parse_view_trace(
        "Viewed: deadbeef\n"
        "Viewed: deadbeef 2026-01-01T10:00:00 open\n"
    ) == [("deadbeef", "2026-01-01T10:00:00", "open")]
@

The trace lives in the trailer commit, so it only reaches a reader if
joining the two halves of a run (\cref{sec:run-join}) carries it
across.
It does, because merging appends the trailer text whole rather than
field by field---which is precisely why that design is worth pinning
down with a test:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_merge_run_trailers_carries_view_trace():
    """A review run's Viewed: lines survive the join."""
    from learnlog.commits import (
        merge_run_trailers,
        parse_view_trace,
    )
    viewed = "c" * 40
    header = (
        "Run: learnlog play\n\nScript: learnlog\n"
        "Arguments: play\nRun-Id: abc123"
    )
    trailers = {
        "abc123": (
            "End-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:20\nExit-Code: 0\n"
            f"Viewed: {viewed} 2026-01-01T10:00:01 open"
        ),
    }
    merged = merge_run_trailers(header, trailers)
    assert parse_view_trace(merged) == [
        (viewed, "2026-01-01T10:00:01", "open"),
    ]
@


\section{Classifying run outcomes}
\label{sec:classify-run-outcomes}

The [[list]] command can filter commits by outcome, which serves
two concrete workflows.  A teacher reviewing a student's log
passes [[--failure]] to jump straight to the runs that went
wrong.  A student who has just broken working code passes
[[--success]] to locate the most recent commit with a working
run, which they can then check out to recover.

Both workflows reduce to the same underlying question: does a
given run block represent a successful run or a failed one?
The [[progsnap2]] module (\cref{progsnap2}) has
[[parse_run_block]] that extracts every field of a block, but
filtering only needs the verdict, not the details---and reaching
into the exporter from the CLI would invert the module layering
established in \cref{commits}.  So we define a narrower
classifier here that the CLI can import without pulling in the
exporter.

A run is a success only when the exit code is zero \emph{and} no
exception was recorded.  The recorder in \cref{learnlog} currently
sets the exit code to [[1]] whenever an exception was captured, so
in practice the two signals always agree---but we check both
deliberately, so the classifier does not rely on that coupling.
If the recorder ever distinguishes a clean exit that nonetheless
logged a problem (warnings, partial failures, data imported from
another tool), requiring both signals keeps the classifier correct
without further changes.

A block without an [[Exit-Code:]] line is classified as
\emph{unknown} rather than \emph{failure}.  This happens when
learnlog is killed (SIGKILL, power loss) before it can commit the
trailer; the run may well have been proceeding
fine when the interruption occurred, so reporting it as a failure
would be misleading.  Leaving such blocks out of both filters is
the safer default---the teacher or student can still see them
with no filter applied.

<<commits functions>>=
def classify_run_block(block_text):
    """Classify a single run block.

    Returns one of ``"success"``, ``"failure"``, or
    ``"unknown"``.  Success requires ``Exit-Code: 0`` and no
    recorded exception.  A missing ``Exit-Code`` line yields
    ``"unknown"`` (the run was interrupted before
    ``finalize_run`` wrote the trailer).
    """
    exit_code = None
    exception_lines = []
    in_exception = False
    for line in block_text.split("\n"):
        if line.startswith("Exit-Code:"):
            exit_code = line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
        elif line.strip() == EXCEPTION_SECTION_MARKER:
            in_exception = True
        elif in_exception:
            exception_lines.append(line)

    if exit_code is None:
        return "unknown"

    exception = "\n".join(exception_lines).strip()
    has_exception = exception and exception != "None"

    if exit_code == "0" and not has_exception:
        return "success"
    return "failure"
@

Let's verify the classifier against the three outcome categories,
covering both failure paths.  A successful run has exit code zero
and an explicit [[None]] in the exception section:

<<commits test functions>>=
